_Remark 1.2_.: As an immediate application of this theorem with \(g=0\), we see that when \(\Omega\) is a bounded convex polygon in \(\mathbb{R}^{2}\) (or polyhedron in \(\mathbb{R}^{3}\)) then the mapping \(u\to\Delta u\) is an isomorphism from \(W^{2\cdot p}(\Omega)\cap W^{1\cdot p}_{0}(\Omega)\) onto \(L^{p}(\Omega)\) for all \(p\!\in\!(1,2+\varepsilon]\) for some \(\varepsilon>0\). When the boundary of \(\Omega\) is \(\mathcal{C}^{1\cdot 1}\), this isomorphism holds for all \(p\!\in\!(1,\infty)\).

